{
	"about": "How Hindi verb forms are read out of the Wiktionary dump and turned into lessons. Written by Claude (Opus) with Ben on 16 November 2026. Hindi verbs change for who and for whether a man or a woman is the one, so a verb's page has a who-by-when grid again, with a man's forms except in the row for she, as Russian's and Polish's have. Most of its forms are two words, a changed verb and a helper (जाता हूँ, जा रहा है). The dump's tags do not tell the habitual from the perfect or the progressive (खाता हूँ, खाया हूँ and खा रहा हूँ carry the same tags), so only what is irregular and unambiguous is read from it: the stem, the past (गया, गई), the future (one word, where खाया हूँगा shares its tags), and the commands. The habitual and the going-on forms are regular, and are made from the stem (`derive`: जा and ता हूँ, जा and रहा हूँ); the first word carries the ending and the second is the helper the board lights. Each form's romanization goes beside it in the tables. The dump spells some endings the older way (खायी, दिये, खाइये); `respell` gives the modern standard spelling the grid's own lessons use (खाई, दिए, खाइए).",
